{
  "id": "80834",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Crystal Field Theory",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The behavior of coordination compounds cannot be adequately explained by the same theories used for main-group element chemistry. The observed geometries of coordination complexes are not consistent with hybridized orbitals on the central metal overlapping with ligand orbitals, as would be predicted by valence bond theory. The observed colors indicate that the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals often occur at different energy levels rather than being of equal energy (as are the three "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "p"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "886668335",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "To explain the behavior of transition metal complexes (such as their colors, magnetic properties, and structures), a model involving electrostatic interactions between the electrons from the ligands and the electrons in the unhybridized "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals of the central metal atom has been developed. This model—known as "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "crystal field theory"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A model that explains the energies of the orbitals in transition metals in terms of electrostatic interactions with the ligands but does not include metal ligand bonding."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2771792524",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "736278941",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " (CFT)—focuses on the nonbonding electrons on the central metal ion in coordination complexes, not on the metal–ligand bonds. Like valence bond theory, CFT tells only part of the story of the behavior of complexes. However, it tells the part that valence bond theory does not. In its pure form, CFT ignores any covalent bonding between ligands and metal ions. Both the ligand and the metal are treated as infinitesimally small point charges."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4079140372",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "All electrons are negative, so the electrons donated from the ligands will repel the electrons of the central metal. For example, let’s consider the behavior of the electrons in the unhybridized "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals in an octahedral complex. The five "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals consist of lobe-shaped regions with spatial arrangements as shown below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3949522241",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "t6p83es3m1kxce8",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 171,
            "id": "2761743779",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/11/11429CFCA853190B11521E051F247C90/dOrbitalsUpdated.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 700
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In an uncomplexed metal ion in the gas phase, the electrons are distributed among the five "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals in accord with "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Hund's rule"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Every orbital in a subshell is singly occupied with one electron before any one orbital is doubly occupied, and all electrons in singly occupied orbitals have the same spin. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3288608761",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "349146329",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " because the orbitals all have the same energy. However, when ligands coordinate to a metal ion, the energies of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals are no longer the same. In octahedral complexes, the lobes in two of the five "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals (the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "x2−y2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "z2"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals) point toward the ligands (represented by L). The other three orbitals (the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "xy"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "xz"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "yz"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals) have lobes that point between the ligands. As six ligands approach the metal ion along the axes of the octahedron, their electrons repel the electrons in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals of the metal ion. However, the repulsions between the electrons in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals that point directly at the ligands are greater than the repulsions between the electrons in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals that point between the ligands. Electrons in “direct-pointing” "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals therefore have higher potential energies than those of electrons in the “in-between-pointing” "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals. The difference in energy may be represented as shown below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "741025666",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The difference in energy between the two types of "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " orbitals is called the crystal field splitting and is symbolized by "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ". In this figure, the subscript "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " stands for octahedral."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "136446483",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "829491515",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "07hnazmlf8tetvi",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 223,
            "id": "2119575881",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/1B/1B5FA6695F65C3B5CB327BC8AA12FE5A/OctahedralSplitting.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 700
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The magnitude of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "crystal field splitting (Δ)"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Splitting the energies of the "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "d"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "-orbitals due to differences in their interactions with ligands."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3410464863",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2777656745",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " depends on many factors, including the nature of the six ligands located around the central metal ion, the charge on the metal, and whether the metal is using 3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": ", 4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": ", or 5"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals. Different ligands produce different crystal field splittings. The increasing crystal field splitting produced by ligands is expressed in the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "spectrochemical series"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A ranking of ligands according to the magnitude of the crystal field splitting they induce."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2221381670",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2710244680",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", a short version of which is given here:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3876246791",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "mczalobpa9hs3wv",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2524773527",
            "src": "\\mathrm I^{-\\;}<\\;\\mathrm{Br}^-\\;<\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-\\;<\\;\\mathrm F^-\\;<\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\;<\\;{\\mathrm C}_2{\\mathrm O}_4^{2-}\\;<\\;{\\mathrm{NH}}_3\\;<\\;\\mathrm{en}\\;<\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_2^-\\;<\\;\\mathrm{CN}^-",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this series, ligands on the left cause small crystal field splittings and are called "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "weak-field ligands"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Ligands that cause small crystal field splittings."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "643774724",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2590342866",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", whereas those on the right cause larger splittings and are "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "strong-field ligands"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Ligands that cause larger crystal field splittings."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1637087096",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1114208201",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Therefore, the Δ"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "oct"
              },
              {
                "text": " value for an octahedral complex with iodide ligands (I"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is much smaller than the Δ"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "oct"
              },
              {
                "text": " value for the same metal with cyanide ligands (CN"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ")."
              }
            ],
            "id": "698683416",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electrons in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals follow the Aufbau (“filling up”) principle, which says that the orbitals will be filled to give the lowest total energy, just as in main-group chemistry. When two electrons occupy the same orbital, the like charges repel each other. The energy needed to pair up two electrons in a single orbital is called the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "pairing energy (P)"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Energy required to place two electrons with opposite spins into a single orbital."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "263990034",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3215804862",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". P is similar in magnitude to Δ"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "oct"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Electrons will always singly occupy each orbital in a degenerate (same energy) set before pairing. When electrons fill the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals, the relative magnitudes of Δ"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "oct"
              },
              {
                "text": " and P determine which orbitals will be occupied."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1774673401",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2901375343",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "492012355",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The electron configuration for Fe"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is [Ar]3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". In an Fe"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion with no ligands, the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " orbitals all have the same energy. The electrons fill the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " orbitals according to Hund’s rule and the Pauli exclusion principle, so one orbital contains a pair of electrons and the other four orbitals each contain one electron, all with the same spin (shown on the left in the image below)."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2037288689",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In [Fe(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", the weak field of the water molecules produces only a small crystal field splitting (Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " < P). Because it requires less energy for the electrons to occupy the higher-energy orbitals than to pair together, there will be an electron in each of the five 3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " orbitals before pairing occurs. For the six "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " electrons on the iron(II) center in [Fe(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", there will be one pair of electrons and four unpaired electrons (shown in the middle of the image). Complexes such as the [Fe(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+ "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "ion, in which the electrons are unpaired because the crystal field splitting is not large enough to cause them to pair, are called "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "high-spin complexes"
                      }
                    ],
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "Complexes in which the electrons maximize the total electron spin by singly populating all of the orbitals before pairing two electrons into the lower-energy orbitals."
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "343358759",
                        "type": "p"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4123700548",
                    "trigger": "hover",
                    "type": "popup"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " because the number of unpaired electrons (spins) is maximized."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2502255424",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In [Fe(CN)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "4−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", on the other hand, the strong field of six cyanide ligands produces a large Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Under these conditions, the electrons require less energy to pair in the lower energy (in-between-pointing) "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " orbitals than they require to be excited to the higher energy (direct-pointing) "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " orbitals (Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " > P). The six 3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " electrons of the Fe"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ion pair in the three lower-energy orbitals (shown in the middle of the image). Complexes in which the electrons are paired because of the large crystal field splitting are called "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "low-spin complexes"
                      }
                    ],
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "Complexes in which the electrons minimize the total electron spin by pairing in the lower-energy orbitals before populating the higher-energy orbitals."
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "3049152815",
                        "type": "p"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4032823799",
                    "trigger": "hover",
                    "type": "popup"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " because the number of unpaired electrons (spins) is minimized."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "950705368",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "caption": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "(a)"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": " Iron(II) complexes have six electrons in the 3d orbitals. In the absence of a crystal field, the orbitals are degenerate. "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "(b)"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": " For coordination complexes with strong-field ligands, such as [Fe(CN)"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "6"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "]"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "4−"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": ", Δ"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "oct"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": " is greater than P, and the electrons pair in the lower-energy orbitals before occupying higher-energy orbitals. "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "(c)"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": " With weak-field ligands such as H"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "O, the ligand field splitting is less than the pairing energy, Δ"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "oct"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": " is less than P, so the electrons occupy all "
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "d"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": " orbitals singly before any pairing occurs."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2788545979",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "dgrqo0dk277ky0w",
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "display": "block",
                "id": "2658262723",
                "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/4A/4A3A54A32C96C1EBA38A7B2536A960B0/Fe_complex_splittings.png",
                "type": "img"
              },
              {
                "children": [
